The original `GetMesg` scans every entry on each call. This version builds a 64K-entry index table once and then answers each lookup with a single array access.

- **Same results:** when several entries share a message number, the table keeps the first index, so the function returns the same entry the linear scan would.
- **Safe initialization:** the table is a function-local static, so C++ initializes it once in a thread-safe way. It is built only from the constant `mesgs` profile data, not from any mutable global state.
- **Fallback:** if `MESGS` were ever too large to fit a 16-bit index, the function falls back to the original linear scan. This branch is resolved at compile time.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    if (MESGS >= 0xFFFF)
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
        {
            if (mesgs[i].num == num)
                return &mesgs[i];
        }
        return NULL;
    }

    static const std::vector<FIT_UINT16> table = []()
    {
        std::vector<FIT_UINT16> t(65536, (FIT_UINT16)0xFFFF);
        for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
        {
            FIT_UINT16 n = mesgs[i].num;
            if (t[n] == (FIT_UINT16)0xFFFF)
                t[n] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
        }
        return t;
    }();

    FIT_UINT16 idx = table[num];
    if (idx == (FIT_UINT16)0xFFFF)
        return NULL;
    return &mesgs[idx];
}
```

